Q: Using Sharp to compress image size without changing aspect ratio of the image A: <p>This is a common problem with <code>Sharp</code> but yet this is a nice &amp; amazing package. Read the <code>Sharp</code> <a href="https://sharp.pixelplumbing.com/api-output#png" rel="nofollow noreferrer">documentation</a> for <code>png</code>.</p>

<p>If you need to play around with;
<code>options.quality</code>, <code>options.colours</code>, <code>options.dither</code> to compress a significant amount of any PNG image and according to its doc, </p>

<blockquote>
  <p><strong>requires libvips compiled with support for libimagequant</strong></p>
</blockquote>

<p>So you need the help of <code>libimagequant</code> to achieve your goal.
According to the site, <strong>libimagequant</strong> is a Small, portable C library for high-quality conversion of RGBA images to 8-bit indexed-color (palette) images.</p>

<p>Instead you can use <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/imagemin" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this NPM PACKAGE called imagemin</a> and I am sure it uses the same method to compress PNG images. With a small piece of code as below, you can reduce the size of PNG.</p>

<pre><code>const files = await imagemin(['images/*.{png}'], {
    destination: 'build/images',
        plugins: [
            imageminJpegtran(),
            imageminPngquant({
                quality: [0.6, 0.8]
            })
        ]
});
</code></pre>

<p>Play around with <code>quality: [0.6, 0.8]</code> like <code>quality: [0.2, 0.2]</code> to reduce the size and you can pass a single image file like <code>const files = await imagemin(['fileName.png']</code></p>

Q: Resizing N # of squares to be as big as possible while still fitting into box of X by Y dimensions. (Thumbnails!) A: <p>There is an O(1) algorithm which solves this problem correctly and can be extended to handle rectangles too. A TypeScript version which returns the side length of the square and the number of rows and columns the grid has:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>type Dimensions = {
    rectWidth: number,
    rectHeight: number,
    rows: number,
    columns: number
}

function gridDimensions(
  numSquares: number,
  width: number,
  height: number,
  rectRatio: number
): Dimensions {
  const ratio = (width / rectRatio) / height
  if (ratio &lt; 1) {
    const d = gridDimensions(numSquares, height, width, 1 / rectRatio)
    return {
      rectWidth: d.rectHeight,
      rectHeight: d.rectWidth,
      rows: d.columns,
      columns: d.rows
    }
  }

  const b0 = Math.sqrt(numSquares / ratio)
  let res: Dimensions = { rectWidth: 0, rectHeight: 0, rows: 0, columns: 0 }

  for (let b = Math.floor(b0); b &lt;= Math.ceil(b0); b++) {
    if (b == 0) {
      continue
    }

    const a = Math.ceil(numSquares / b)
    const s = Math.min((width / rectRatio) / a, height / b)

    if (s &gt; res.rectHeight) {
      res = { rectWidth: s * rectRatio, rectHeight: s, rows: b, columns: a }
    }
  }

  return res
}
</code></pre>
<p>A short version which works only with squares and returns just the side length, which better highlights the structure of the solution, is:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function maximalSquareSideLength(n, w, h) {
  const r = w / r
  if (r &lt; 1) return maximalSquareSideLength(n, h, w)
  const s = (b) =&gt; Math.min(w / Math.ceil(n / b), h / b)
  const b0 = Math.sqrt(n / r)
  return Math.max(s(Math.floor(b0)), s(Math.ceil(b0)))
}
</code></pre>
<p>The code above elides the <code>b == 0</code> check since <code>s(0) = Math.min(w / Inf, Inf) = Math.min(0, Inf) = 0</code>, becoming effectively a no-op. Re-add the condition back if in your environment division by zero raises an exception.</p>
<p>This solution exploits two particularities of the problem:</p>
<ol>
<li>It is symmetric with respect to the ratio of box dimensions: solving for a wide box with ratio <code>r</code> gives the same solution as for a tall box with ratio <code>1/r</code> but with flipped dimensions – rows become columns, columns become rows; it's just a rotation by 90 degrees</li>
<li>It's easy to see that the ideal number of rows must be around <code>sqrt(n * h/w)</code>; it can be further proven that for <code>r = w/h &gt;= 1</code> the optimal solution will always be one of <code>{ floor(b0), ceil(b0) }</code> with <code>b0 = sqrt(n * h/w)</code>.</li>
</ol>
<p><a href="https://math.stackexchange.com/a/5138148/1761953">My Mathematics Stack Exchange answer</a> proves this result.</p>
